# ML engineering efficiency

When planning or building ML work, optimize for **clean code**, **algorithmic efficiency**, and **right-sized tooling**.

## Clean code (data/ML)

- **Package over notebook** for prod; functions do one thing; type hints on public APIs
- **No repeated full-frame copies** — chain transforms; `.copy()` only when mutating
- **Explicit dtypes** — `category`, `bool`, `float32` when it cuts memory
- **Name by domain** — `prob_mat`, `spread_7d`, not `df2`
- **Promote before optimizing** — readable vectorized pandas beats clever nested loops

## Big-O sanity check

Before nested loops or `itertools.combinations`, state expected **n** and complexity:

| Pattern | Typical complexity | Prefer when |
|---------|-------------------|-------------|
| `pivot_table` / `groupby` | O(n) | Aggregations |
| Dict/set lookup | O(1) per key | Joins, `(partner, indicator)` maps |
| `DataFrame.corr` | O(d² × rows) | d = partners/columns (small d OK) |
| Nested loops over indicators × partners | O(I × P²) | **Avoid** — vectorize or groupby |
| Pairwise combinations per group | O(k²) per group | Cap k or filter first |

If I×P or Σk² is > ~1M ops, refactor before adding features.

## Promote to core (on cutover only)

- **Notebooks explore** — keep cleaning/featuring inline in `notebooks/` or `htoc_ml/analysis/`; do not extract to `htoc.core` mid-exploration
- **On promotion** to `htoc_ml/src/htoc/` or `htoc.datapipelines/` — move **reusable** cleaning and feature transforms into `htoc.core` with pytest; model-specific logic stays in `htoc.{noi,prism,...}`; datapipelines wire core, they don't own transforms

